    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-320646",
      "knowledge_point_id": "9e89b620-641a-5dbf-85f6-ca0f7a2fad80",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Arcsine or Arccosine",
      "problem": "Expressed in radians, $\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "The range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arccos}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n0\\le \\theta \\le \\pi .\n$$\n\nNote that the angle $\\theta $ spans the first and second quadrants.\n\nSince $-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}$ is negative, our answer is an angle that lies in the second quadrant, where cosine is negative.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the second quadrant, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{6})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arccos}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=\\frac{5\\pi }{6}.\n$$",
