    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-321120",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required",
      "problem": "Expressed in radians, $9\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=${{field-1}}.",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-\\frac{\\pi }{2}\\le \\theta \\le \\frac{\\pi }{2}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/a33ea7c6d1aa7cd7e54376aefb2af4347da59a61ecaabdbf4964d41b9661870a.png)\n\nFrom the values in the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{5\\pi }{3})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with $\\frac{5\\pi }{3}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract $2\\pi .$\n\n$$\n\\frac{5\\pi }{3}-2\\pi =-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\narcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2})=-\\frac{\\pi }{3}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}9arcsin\\,(-\\frac{\\sqrt{3}}{2}) & =9(-\\frac{\\pi }{3}) \\\\  & =-3\\pi .\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "choices": [
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "-3\\pi"
            },
            {
              "type": "math",
              "value": "3\\pi"
            }
          ],
          "correct_value": "-3\\pi"
        }
