    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-320893",
      "knowledge_point_id": "7c2791d4-f801-5555-a859-1d373f91a67e",
      "knowledge_point": "Evaluating Arcsine When a Coterminal Angle is Required",
      "problem": "Expressed in degrees, $4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=${{field-1}}${}^{∘}.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/ca39c4cde40b99fd40a11746a31398d82814268b2df706e3101ee88648c9e2d4.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({315}^{∘})=-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${315}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${315}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{315}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2})=-{45}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}4\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-\\frac{\\sqrt{2}}{2}) & =4\\cdot (-{45}^{∘}) \\\\  & ={-180}^{∘}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
