      "worked_solution": "First, note that the range of $\\theta =\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡x$ is given by\n\n$$\n-{90}^{∘}\\le \\theta \\le {90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nNow, let's take a look at the unit circle.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13964047/assets/d93c490adbb5cdbc0e9a09b60eea9b82ab93be8e871e334423abc17ddd62955e.png)\n\nFrom the values on the unit circle, we see that\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}⁡({270}^{∘})=-1.\n$$\n\nHowever, ${270}^{∘}$ does not lie within the range of $\\theta .$ To get an angle that's coterminal with ${270}^{∘}$ that lies within the range of $\\theta ,$ we subtract ${360}^{∘}.$\n\n$$\n{270}^{∘}-{360}^{∘}=-{90}^{∘}.\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{arcsin}⁡(-1)={-90}^{∘}.\n$$",
